.SH "NAME"
highlight \- colorize regexp matches read from standard input
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
\fBhighlight\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIPATTERN0\fR [\fIPATTERN1\fR \&...]
.sp
\fBhighlight\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-filter\fR \fICOLOR,PATTERN\fR [\fI\-\-filter\fR \fICOLOR,PATTERN\fR \&...]
.sp
\fBhighlight\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
\fBhighlight\fR reads text from standard input, one line at a time, and writes it to standard output with every occurrence of the given patterns wrapped in ANSI 256\-color escape sequences\&. Patterns are interpreted as Perl regular expressions\&.
.sp
When patterns are given as bare arguments (\fIPATTERN0\fR, \fIPATTERN1\fR, \&...) they are colored using a built\-in cycle of ten default colors, assigned in order\&. When a pattern is supplied with the \fB\-\-filter\fR option, the color may be chosen explicitly by prefixing the pattern with a color specification and a comma (see COLOR SPECIFICATION)\&.
.sp
By default matching uses \fBsmartcase\fR: a pattern containing at least one uppercase letter is matched case\-sensitively, while an all\-lowercase pattern is matched case\-insensitively\&. This behavior can be overridden with \fB\-\-case_sensitive\fR or \fB\-\-case_insensitive\fR\&.
.sp
If a pattern contains capturing parentheses, only the text captured by the groups is colorized, not the entire match\&. Use \fB\-\-full_matches\fR to colorize the whole match instead\&.
.sp
A 256\-color terminal is required for color output\&. When output is not connected to a terminal (for example, in a pipeline or a test harness), colorization is disabled unless \fB\-\-force\fR is given\&.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-bold\fR
.RS 4
Render the matched patterns in a bold font, in addition to coloring them\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-case_insensitive\fR
.RS 4
Perform case\-insensitive matching\&. Equivalent to the smartcase behavior for all\-lowercase patterns, applied to every pattern\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-case_sensitive\fR
.RS 4
Perform case\-sensitive matching for every pattern, disabling smartcase\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-f\fR \fICOLOR,PATTERN\fR, \fB\-\-filter\fR \fICOLOR,PATTERN\fR
.RS 4
Add a single pattern together with its color\&. The color and the pattern are separated by the first comma\&. This form must be used when a pattern begins with a hyphen, otherwise it would be parsed as an option\&. May be given multiple times\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
.RS 4
Read additional patterns from
\fIFILE\fR, one entry per line\&. Blank lines and lines beginning with
#
are ignored\&. A line containing only a pattern uses the next color in the default cycle; a line containing a color specification and a pattern separated by one or more tab characters uses that color\&. This option may be combined with patterns given on the command line\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-full_matches\fR
.RS 4
Colorize the entire match even when the pattern contains capturing parentheses\&. By default only captured groups are colorized\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-force\fR
.RS 4
Force colorization on at all times, even when standard output is not a terminal and even when the
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
environment variable is set\&. This is primarily useful in pipelines and tests\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-debug\fR
.RS 4
Print the list of patterns and their assigned colors to standard output before processing input\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-help\fR
.RS 4
Print a short usage message and exit\&.
.RE
.SH "COLOR SPECIFICATION"
.sp
A color specification is a string describing one or more ANSI attributes\&. The following forms are understood:
.PP
Numeric ANSI color
.RS 4
Any 256\-color palette index, for example
33
or
214\&.
.RE
.PP
Named color
.RS 4
One of
red
(160),
blue
(27),
green
(34),
yellow
(226),
orange
(214),
purple
(93),
white
(15), or
black
(0)\&.
.RE
.PP
True color
.RS 4
A hexadecimal value in the form
#RRGGBB
or just
RRGGBB
after a
#, specifying a 24\-bit foreground color\&.
.RE
.sp
Multiple attributes may be chained with underscores (_):
.PP
COLOR_bold
.RS 4
Combine a color with bold, for example
165_bold\&.
.RE
.PP
COLOR_on_BACKGROUND
.RS 4
Set a foreground and a background color, for example
10_on_140
or
white_on_blue\&. The literal prefix
on_
denotes the following color as a background\&.
.RE
.sp
Attribute keywords recognized on their own or when chained include bold (1), italic (3), underline (4), blink (5), and inverse (7)\&. The keyword reset emits an ANSI reset sequence\&.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.PP
\fBHIGHLIGHT_COLORS\fR
.RS 4
A comma\-separated list of ANSI color numbers used to replace the built\-in default color cycle\&. For example,
HIGHLIGHT_COLORS=160,27
assigns
160
to the first bare pattern,
27
to the second, and so on\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
.RS 4
When set to a non\-empty value, disables color output\&. Ignored when
\fB\-\-force\fR
is given\&.
.RE
.SH "FILES"
.sp
No configuration files are read\&.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.sp
Highlight the word error in a log stream using the default color:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
tail \-f app\&.log | highlight error
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
Highlight errors in red and warnings in yellow, case\-insensitively:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
highlight \-\-case_insensitive \-\-filter red,error \-\-filter yellow,warning
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
Colorize only the captured user name in a log line:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
tail \-f auth\&.log | highlight \*(Aquser: (\ew+)\*(Aq
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
Highlight a pattern that begins with a hyphen:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
highlight \-\-filter \*(Aq196,\-v\*(Aq
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
Use a pattern file containing tab\-separated color and pattern entries:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
highlight \-\-file patterns\&.txt
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
\fB0\fR
.RS 4
Success, or
\fB\-\-help\fR
was requested\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB255\fR
.RS 4
An invalid option was supplied, or no patterns were supplied\&. A usage message is printed before exiting\&.
.RE
